# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
knowing	one
ignorant	one
wise	one
good	one
bad	one
overlike	two	more	P1 P9 P10	outdo another in a craft's practice (exceed in saying or doing)	take a larger share or advantage over another (pleonexia)
overunlike	two	more	P1 P9 P10	outdo another in a craft's practice (exceed in saying or doing)	take a larger share or advantage over another (pleonexia)
like	one
bridge	A2	more	outdo in a craft's practice (the wise and the ignorant)	take a larger share or advantage (the just and the unjust)
# In P5, P6, C1 and C2, "more" comes from the arts: the musician tightening strings, the physician prescribing, the knower "saying or doing" more.
# In S1, S39 and S42 it is Thrasymachus's grasping for gain: having more than others.
# A2 equates the just's or unjust's desire with the wise's or ignorant's desire. That match carries "more" from one sense to the other.
# I was unsure about "wise"/"good" in P2 and P3 (S3, S5). There Thrasymachus means shrewd or advantage-securing, while S12-S13 and S31-S33 mean skilled or knowing. Their statement texts were not given, so I left these claims as one sense.
